        {
          "id": "fact_002",
          "title": "Bratislava Calvary: the Baroque pilgrimage complex that almost disappeared",
          "text": "Bratislava Calvary was among the early Baroque Stations of the Cross in the Kingdom of Hungary. Its foundation stone was laid on 27 May 1694 and the complex was completed in 1725. The original design comprised seven main Calvary chapels and two preparatory chapels, guiding pilgrims up the slope to the Crucifixion scene and then to the Holy Sepulchre. The complex was gradually destroyed in the 1950s. The construction of Pražská Street caused especially severe damage: part of the hill was cut away and further chapels disappeared. Only sections of the original route and the remains of the Ecce Homo chapel survive. Restoration in recent decades has revived part of the site's religious role: the terrace by the cross and one chapel have been restored, and contemporary images of the Stations have been placed along the historic route. Calvary therefore recalls both Baroque pilgrimage culture and the loss of urban heritage in the 20th century."
        },
        {
          "id": "fact_003",
          "title": "Hlboká cesta: an old toll road beside quarries and springs",
          "text": "Hlboká cesta is one of the historic routes on the north-western edge of old Bratislava. Its predecessor is documented by the late 15th century; the road formed part of the city's outer customs and toll line and led from the town towards Lamač and the Záhorie region. Stone quarried nearby was used for roads, fortifications and houses, while springs along the route contributed to the city's water supply. The section between today's Čapkova and Havlíčkova streets was originally a narrow woodland path cut into rock and enclosed by steep slopes. In 1870 mayor Henrich Justi had it widened at the suggestion of the Bratislava Beautification Society. Better access encouraged the development of a villa district between Calvary and Horský park and opened the woodland park to residents for recreation. Hlboká cesta is therefore more than a road: it records how the rocky edge of the city became a residential and recreational landscape."
        },
        {
          "id": "fact_004",
          "title": "Prüger-Wallner Garden: a villa, a landscaped garden and historic greenhouses",
          "text": "Prüger-Wallner Garden was created in the late 19th century around the villa of the Prüger-Wallner family at the northern end of today's Havlíčkova Street. Its sloping terrain was arranged as a formal ornamental landscape garden near the villa and a productive garden where flowers, fruit and vegetables were grown. Greenhouses and a boiler house stood in the southern part. After the Second World War the garden gradually fell into neglect; much of its original design disappeared and the productive area was divided into smaller garden plots. Some historic features and mature trees survived. Bratislava later began restoring the site with partners. Today it is once again a public garden, with restored historic greenhouses, wetland features and community spaces. It shows how private gardens in the former villa district were designed and how their surviving elements can be carefully returned to urban life."
        },
        {
          "id": "fact_005",
          "title": "The Lourdes Grotto: a Marian shrine in a former quarry",
          "text": "The Lourdes Grotto was created in 1892 in a disused quarry below the former Chapel of Our Lady of the Snows. The initiative came from Bratislava resident Countess Helena Szapáry, with help from local people; a statue of Our Lady of Lourdes was placed in the adapted grotto. The site continued the older pilgrimage tradition of Calvary and the chapel that once stood above the quarry. Its design imitates the famous grotto at Lourdes in France, while preserving a distinct local trace of stone quarrying: a place once used to extract building material became a small pilgrimage site. The grotto lies by Hlboká cesta, close to the Church of Our Lady of the Snows. It illustrates how the natural terrain, the economic use of the hillside and religious tradition became intertwined here."
        },
        {
          "id": "fact_006",
          "title": "The Church of Our Lady of the Snows: a wartime building on an older pilgrimage site",
          "text": "The Church of Our Lady of the Snows on Calvary is a prominent religious building erected between 1943 and 1948 on the site of an older chapel. A predecessor dedicated to Our Lady of the Snows stood here in the first half of the 19th century and was linked to the pilgrimage tradition of Calvary and the nearby Lourdes Grotto. The new church was designed by architects Fuchs, Vécsei and Quastler. Its massive reinforced-concrete structure contrasts with the historic chapels and villas nearby. It originally had a tall tower, which was removed in 1958. The name Calvary gradually came to designate the surrounding parish area as well; the parish itself was established in 1933. The church thus connects an older Marian pilgrimage site with Bratislava's architecture and religious life in the 20th century."
        },
        {
          "id": "fact_007",
          "title": "The occupation of Bratislava in 1919: the main station at a turning point in statehood",
          "text": "During the night of 31 December 1918 to 1 January 1919, Czechoslovak troops occupied Bratislava’s main railway station. The following day they entered the city and assumed military control. The event marked a decisive turning point in the history of former Pressburg, which became part of the new Czechoslovakia after the break-up of Austria-Hungary. The takeover was not merely a formal change of borders: it was accompanied by tensions among local residents, Hungarian troops and the new military administration. The main station recalls the moment when the railway hub became one of the key sites in Bratislava’s transition to the Czechoslovak state."
        }
      ]
